<p>The Pentagon on May 8 began releasing previously classified and newly public files on unidentified anomalous phenomena, or UAPs. The release followed President Trump’s February directive to federal agencies to identify and disclose government records related to UAPs, extraterrestrial life and related subjects. The first release included 162 files posted to a new government website. The material came from the Defense Department, FBI, NASA and State Department and included documents, photographs and videos tied to reported sightings dating back decades. Officials described the release as a transparency effort and said the public could draw its own conclusions from the material. President Trump wrote that the disclosure was aimed at “Complete and Maximum Transparency.” Some reports said analysts noted that many of the documents were already public. Several reports said the archive contains unusual sightings reported by pilots, astronauts and military personnel but does not provide definitive proof of alien life.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/k5nmChRy1X-oBg90bCF4622NYpbZEEVES0ugMo6V4nh1G2KL1kPQ9M44MGxB_Ad9GxnQMJmDwPRbhOKUBjUzYTXvwDp8OuQET6ilxOnzHf6quep1kQHMMJdCDncH8cNlw7ftPJ5tVE5qLFhbkQwwEeEoDLHFMdgnYuIjRD7BkT3wZfgVKtjXn6yANJnxNwSWirf2pChet0xnKFpRFJSWkbTEAYPNF6p5ZXnU2-heXLMnAS0IKqw_PmPQ9bGfKB9qlvSFtHcJuJlOkom_' alt='Infrared still image released by the U.S. Department of Defense and FBI showing unidentified object(s) over the western United States in September 2025.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>File contents: CBS News reported that the first release contained 120 PDFs, 28 videos and 14 image files, including still images from military aircraft footage of objects.</li><li>Orange orb report: A 2023 document cited by Teletica said three federal special agents separately reported seeing orange orbs that emitted smaller red orbs in the sky.</li><li>Further releases: Officials said they would post additional UAP documents on a rolling basis after the first tranche.</li></ul><blockquote>These files, hidden behind classifications, have long fueled justified speculation — and it&#x27;s time the American people see it for themselves. - Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth: Hegseth said the Defense Department was “in lockstep” with President Trump to bring “unprecedented transparency” regarding the government’s understanding of UAPs. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/pentagon-begins-release-ufo-files/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>President Trump: Trump contrasted the disclosure with past administrations, invited people to review the files and decide for themselves, and ended his Truth Social post with “Have Fun and Enjoy!” (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/pentagon-begins-release-ufo-files/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Pentagon: The department said the materials had been reviewed for security purposes, but many had “not yet been analyzed for resolution of any anomalies.” (<a href='https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/08/pentagon-releases-ufo-files-on-new-website/'>TechCrunch</a>)</li><li>Skeptics cited by Scientific American: Scientific American reported that skeptical observers were underwhelmed by the release and did not view the trove as settling claims about extraordinary phenomena. (<a href='https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/pentagon-releases-trove-of-new-ufo-files-but-skeptics-arent-impressed/'>Scientific American</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/us-pentagon-releases-trove-of-classified-ufo-files/a-77100163'>US: Pentagon releases trove of classified UFO files</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/us-politics/article-pentagon-releases-ufo-files/'>Pentagon releases previously classified files on alleged UFO sightings</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://japantoday.com/category/world/pentagon-begins-releasing-new-files-on-ufos-and-says-the-public-can-draw-i